    {
      "title": "'Recreate Responsibly' Trailhead Program",
      "description": "<p><span data-olk-copy-source=\"MessageBody\">Learn to \"Recreate Responsibly\" as a visitor at the C&amp;O Canal National Historic Park at one of our tabletop programs!</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span data-olk-copy-source=\"MessageBody\">If you find us at the Olmstead Trailhead on your way to the Overlook, learn about Washington D.C.'s history with cholera and E. Coli outbreaks and learn skills to ensure you have safe drinking water while recreating anywhere without access to potable water. If you find us at the River Trailhead, this program will focus on the Leave No Trace and Pack In &amp; Out principles employed by the park. As an effort to respect cultural and natural resources, and leaving wildlife undisturbed, these principles are essential to responsible recreation at the C&amp;O Canal.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span data-olk-copy-source=\"MessageBody\">Please note that this program may be modified or cancelled due to weather. </span></p>",
      "category": "Regular Event",
      "tags": [],
      "location": "",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "",
        "longitude": ""
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "",
        "times": "All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "Sustainable Fishing at C&O Canal",
      "description": "<p>Join us at the Great Falls Tavern Visitor Center for our Sustainable Fishing program! Learn about the diverse species of fish that call the canal and the Potomac River home, how to practice responsible and sustainable fishing, and try your hand at casting a rod.   </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Please note that this program may be modified or cancelled due to weather. </p>",
      "category": "Regular Event",
      "tags": [],
      "location": "",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "",
        "longitude": ""
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "2025-07-13, 2025-07-20",
        "times": "All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "Meet the Mules",
      "description": "<p>We're excited to announce that our 'Meet the Mules' program will be kicking off (see what we did there?) this week! 🐎</p>\n<p>Come on out and meet our two <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/choh/learn/historyculture/mules.htm\">working mules</a>, Jen and Julie! Both Jen and Julie provide a unique opportunity for visitors to have a physical connection to the history of the C&amp;O Canal, as well as the story of the United States. Mules were considered the \"engines\" of the canal because they pulled boats on the canal between Georgetown and Cumberland. By visiting the mules, you can learn more about how mules worked to build and operate the canal, how the park cares for Jen and Julie, and some fun facts along the way.</p>\n<p>The program will take place at the mule pen behind the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/choh/planyourvisit/greatfallstavernvisitorcenter.htm\">Great Falls Tavern Visitor Center</a> from 9:30 am - 11:30 am.</p>",
      "category": "Regular Event",
      "tags": [
        "mules",
        "canal",
        "c&o canals",
        "labor history"
      ],
      "location": "",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "",
        "longitude": ""
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "",
        "times": "All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "'Ranger's Choice' Program",
      "description": "<p>Join us at the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/choh/planyourvisit/greatfallstavernvisitorcenter.htm\">Great Falls Tavern Visitor Center</a> for a ranger program and learn more about the history of the C&amp;O Canal! A ranger will present a program with accompanying hands-on learning activities on topics including 19th-century fashion, the history of National Park Service rangers, period music, and more! </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The program will take place between 1:00 pm - 4:00 pm and may be cancelled or modified due to weather.</p>",
      "category": "Regular Event",
      "tags": [],
      "location": "",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "",
        "longitude": ""
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "",
        "times": "All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "Celebrating Juneteenth",
      "description": "<p>10:00 AM: “The Fire of Slavery and Freedom:” Antietam and Emancipation</p>\n<p>Join a park ranger at Antietam National Battlefield to learn about the connection between the Battle of Antietam and emancipation. This program will last about 30 minutes and meet in the orientation room.</p>\n<p>11:00 AM-1:00 PM: Tolson’s Chapel and Juneteenth: The Details and Dedication</p>\n<p>Join members of the Friends of Tolson’s Chapel to learn about the origins of Juneteeth, the establishment of Tolson’s Chapel, the use of the Chapel as a Freedman’s Bureau School during Reconstruction, and the role of the Chapel among Sharpsburg’s African American community. Program is a drop-in and meets at Tolson’s Chapel and School, 111 High St., Sharpsburg, MD, 21782.</p>",
      "category": "Special Event",
      "tags": [],
      "location": "",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "",
        "longitude": ""
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "",
        "times": "All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "Children's Book Reading and Story Game Activity",
      "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span data-olk-copy-source=\"MessageBody\">Join the author for a reading of the children's picture book <em>For a Soldier at Antietam </em>at the Visitor Center followed by a short question &amp; answer session. This book honors the courage of the soldiers buried at Antietam and invites readers of all ages to reflect on the importance of remembering their sacrifices. </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Following the reading, families will be invited to drive to Antietam National Cemetery to walk through the iron gates, wave at the flag, see the stones, say “hello” to Old Simon, and visit the unknown soldier's grave. In addition, children will have the opportunity to create their own soldier story. The program, including the reading and National Cemetery visit, will last approximately one hour.</p>",
      "category": "Special Event",
      "tags": [],
      "location": "",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "",
        "longitude": ""
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "",
        "times": "All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "Cushwa Basin Launch Boat Tour",
      "description": "<p>Join us for a tour on a replica launch boat and learn about the history of the C&amp;O Canal.</p>\n<p>During the late 19th and early 20th century, 30-foot long launch boats traveled the canal for leisure and business, contrasting greatly with the 90 foot mule-drawn cargo boats that usually dominated the canal.</p>\n<p>Tours are limited to 10 people.</p>\n<p>Animals are not allowed, with the exception of service animals.</p>\n<p>Inclement weather, including high winds and extreme heat, may cause tours to be cancelled.</p>",
      "category": "Regular Event",
      "tags": [],
      "location": "205 West Potomac St.\nWilliamsport, MD 21795",
      "coordinates": {
        "latitude": "39.600562",
        "longitude": "-77.826263"
      },
      "dateTime": {
        "dates": "2025-07-12, 2025-07-19, 2025-07-26",
        "times": "All day, All day, All day, All day"
      },
      "contactInfo": {}
    },
    {
      "title": "Meet the Mules",
      "description": "<p>We're excited to announce that our 'Meet the Mules' program will be kicking off (see what we did there?) this week! 🐎</p>\n<p>Come on out and meet our two <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/choh/learn/historyculture/mules.htm\">working mules</a>, Jen and Julie! Both Jen and Julie provide a unique opportunity for visitors to have a physical connection to the history of the C&amp;O Canal, as well as the story of the United States. Mules were considered the \"engines\" of the canal because they pulled boats on the canal between Georgetown and Cumberland. By visiting the mules, you can learn more about how mules worked to build and operate the canal, how the park cares for Jen and Julie, and some fun facts along the way.</p>\n<p>The program will take place at the mule pen behind the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/choh/planyourvisit/greatfallstavernvisitorcenter.htm\">Great Falls Tavern Visitor Center</a> from 9:30 am - 11:30 am.</p>",
